A||||31||RaidenCobra||So, I Need A Crash Course In Shifting.......||||7||
Z||000000||RaidenCobra||05-18-2001||01:27 AM||||Ok, I know you might thing I am dumb or something but everyone has to start somewhere<BR>I need a crash course in shifting and how to "properly" drive a standard..<BR>I was reading previous threads where people were talking about matching ur RPM when downshifting or something and about pressing the clutch in when turning but I have no Idea what this means..(I know about pressing the clutch in =/ )<BR>All I need is a good explanation on it and maybe some techniques and tips.  I know that U release the clutch until a point where it engages the engine and your off, but I have no idea what's up after that.. I tried driving my G/f's cavalier and I was doing alright until I had to stop on a hill..  Then I stalled it and couldn't get it going again...<BR>And like when U approach a light what do U do and when stopped in traffic and all that nice stuff.  I consider myself a skilled driver with auto's so I don't need to learn how to drive..  Just how to shift!<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000001||Sootah||05-18-2001||01:44 AM||||Let off of the clutch until you feel the car start to catch, then apply slightly more gas than you are letting off clutch. I really hate it when people say "push in the gas and let off the clutch equal amounts" that never works. Every cars transmission / clutch is different feeling. This is the best universal way of gettin out o there smoothly.<P>---------<BR>If you don't want them to know, tell them you have herpies||||reg||3
Z||000002||Slammed Fiero||05-18-2001||01:54 AM||||I don't know the exact term for it, But i learned this at Boundrant when I was 16. Basically it is done so that you do not upset the balance of the vehicle. This is particularly important in a mid engine car that can get squirley upon deceleration and turning (Trailing throttle oversteer)<P>Basically when approacing a turn , Put the clutch in and rev the motor to the RPM on the lower Gear. I'm not sure off the top of my head since this motion has become a part of my daily routiene, but I believe that in a 4spd Fiero you should make sure the RPM's are at or lower than 3000rpms , again I could be wrong I do it all basically by ear.<P><BR>So..Driving in 3rd gear , Press the clutch in , right after in a fluid motion rev the motor to the RPM of the next gear. To do it right everytime takes some practice , the Fiero gearbox is notchy in general (all of them are) and the tall shifter does not make it wany easier. With my homemade short shifter , I do not have to remove my elbow from the console to shift.<P>All I can say is practice, it's one of those things that takes time. Don't practice with people in the car they will think you don't know how to drive (until you get it right)<P>If your new to driving a stick don't worry about this , you don't need it for street driving at all. As for sitting on a hill I just use the clutch to get the car to stay put sometimes , probably not good for it. THE brake is the best way, it all boils down to practice , practice taking off smoothly , make sure to practice in a car that is cheap to replace the clutch on!!<P>2 years ago I taught an old girlfriend to drive standard. We went down a back road and she was terrible. The next day driving to work (I worked in the accounting office of a Pontiac dealership) I was driving up a hil shifted into thrird ...won't go , tried to shift back into second ..no go . I coasted to a stop over the hill at a stop light. called the tow truck and waited. When they got it back to the dealership , and a few days later got the tranny out , my clutch was in several pieces. $1200 later i had a new clutch.<P>The moral of the story , make sure it's a cheap car to replace the clutch on. If you don't feel comfortable driving a std , take a break , most people I have taught tend to get frustrated and just munch the tranny more<P><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">     <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals..Autocrossed , Tracked , abused.||||reg||1
Z||000003||RaidenCobra||05-18-2001||02:56 AM||||Well, the car I will be using is an 86 GT 2.8<BR>(don't know if it's 86 and a half or not)<BR>And i dont get frustrated easily, especially when it comes to driving. <BR>I <B>Will</B> be taking it slow for a while just to get the hang of it. <BR>A couple more things.. Do u have u press the clutch when u brake? How far in do u press?<BR>I've heard that in some cases u just lighty press the clutch and in others u have u StEp on it.. Hope people can help since i dont have a whole lot of money and i would like to keep the new clutch thats being installed for a little while at least =D<P>Im looking to find answers for the whole 9 yards on clutching||||reg||1
Z||000004||Old Lar||05-18-2001||05:22 AM||||When stopping you disengage the clutch (push it in).  If you are just slowing down, leave the clutch engaged.  You can drive at 1500 rpm in any gear.  With a manual transmission, there is no slipping (if all is working), so if you come to a complete stop, the car will stall unless you depress the clutch.  The engine doesn't have enough rpms to keep moving.<P>With todays automatic transmissions, some people drive with two feet, one for the gas, one for the brake.  You see them on the road all the time, with one foot resting on the brake pedal, the brake lights are on, off, on, off. <P>With the manual transmission the left foot is for the clutch, right foot for the gas and brakes.<P>As others have said, practice, don't try speed shifting, just take it slow and make sure that the clutch is all the way in when you shift up or down.<P>If you have an 86 GT with a manual transmission, the early 86 had a four speed transmission, the 86 1/2 has a five speed. I think there were only 2000 86 1/2 cars produced.||||reg||3
Z||000005||FieroFanaticus2||05-18-2001||08:27 AM||||Push the gas down as far as it goes, when the engine revs to about 5000 rpm's let the clutch out as fast as you can.  Do this for every gear you shift into.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>BTW -- I hope this is an OBVIOUS joke.||||reg||1
Z||000006||84Bill||05-18-2001||08:45 AM||||Heheh on a hill huh.. Well this is how I learned to drive a standard shift. My friend and I were out crusing the town when we started talking about driving and cars and the like well I mentioned that I couldnt drive a stick and my friend says "do you want to learn how?" Sure I said well he drives out to the steapest hill in town and stops near the top, jumps out and runs around the car and tells me to get in and drive!!! GULP!! well about 10 stalls and 30 feet back down the hill I finally got it. Well dont you know there is a stop sign at the top of the hill!! this time he shows me the emergency brake trick.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> about 9000 rpm and dragging the rear wheels and smoking the front LOL! but I did it without stalling. that was 20 years ago and still remember it like yesterday.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroLisa||05-18-2001||08:52 AM||||Me learning how to drive a stick was a funny thing, I think I almost dumped my boyfriend at the time over it. He lived on a road that was shaped like a U, with 2 hills going up on either side, he lived at the bottom of the hill. The entire road was his family so not much traffic.<BR>He made me get in the car and just practice starting uphill forever. Thought I was gonna kill him. It took me about 2 weeks to finally drive on a real road, and that was the scarist experience of my life...probably because I wasn't actually thinking about what I was actually doing, just driving (sorta). <BR>Anyway I cant really give you any specific tips since every car is different, but practice is what you need to do and you'll begin to feel everything and not ever need to look down at your tach!<P>When I first learned it was on a Hyundai Excel- I thought it was a great car to learn on. But before I ever mastered it, I bought my 5 speed coupe and it had a bad tranny AKA terrible car to try to learn to drive stick. a||||reg||1
Z||000008||thomas_l||05-18-2001||09:45 AM||||Ahhhh... today's spoiled kids...500 channels of satellite TV, instant gratification via the Internet, and learning to drive in the automatic family truckster... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Really tho - just try to focus on being coordinated and smooth. The manual is like walking and chewing gum together. You have to be able to multi-task without consciously thinking about it. Practice practice practice until you can simulate an automatic trans with your smoothness. Don't wuss out with the parking brake on hills either. You should be quick enough to move out and only roll back an inch or two without riding the clutch either, just not today. Lots of practice. BTW, the Fiero is not a good beginner car so don't feel so bad about stalling or grinding a gear now and then. I had the good fortune to learn to drive in a '69 GMC pickup. It had the 235 6cyl w/granny-low 4 speed and no power anything. By the time I was taking the driving test, my dad had me able to parallel park 2" off the curb (same truck) with a trailer attached. Of course I hated his guts after 8 zillion attempts getting it right but I don't regret it. Getting the skill developed at that level made it alot easier for me to hop into just about anything with a manual, from motorcycles to 18 wheelers.<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000009||RacinRob||05-18-2001||10:24 AM||||The way I learned and I think it helped was that I have to get the car rolling and driving with letting the clutch out, without using any gas.  yes it's a little hard on the clutch.  BUT! you learn a lot about the "feel" of the car.  And yes it can be done without killing it, I've done it in the fiero a number of times.  And I make everyone that I try and teach stick to do it to get the feeling of it.  (PS I can't belive the clutch in my cavalier is still good, I taught myself and about 9 girls how to drive stick on it HEHE)  Well after you can start out without gas, start out with gas, start letting the clutch out as if you weren't going to give it gas but then when the rpms go down, give it enough gas to stay at 800-1000 rpms. once you get that down try doing it all a little faster... once your pretty fast at that then go to a hill and put what you learned to the test on  a hill. it took me 40-50 time trying to start on a hill and about a month before I was comfortable driving my cavalier... see the biggest motivation for me to learn was that I bought a stick and it was the only car I could drive so I wanted to learn quick... I still remember the the first night I bought the car I buried the Speedo at 100 mph.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ken Wittlief||05-18-2001||10:38 AM||||Here is the straight scoop - a little understanding goes a long way.<P>First of all, your one question, whenever you stop you need to press the clutch all the way to the floor, and everytime you shift - all the way to the floor.<P>when your car is not moving and the engine is running and you got it in first gear, the flywheel is spinning about 900rpm and the clutch plate is not spinning at all.<P> What you are doing when you let the clutch pedal up is applying a friction force to the clutch from the flywheel, to bring it up to 900 rpm, same as the engine (flywheel).<P>  Since the clutch plate is attached to the tranny, which is attached to the car, you cant simply let the clutch out instantly <P> because: with the engine running at 900rpm and with the clutch engaged (pedal up) in first gear, the car would be going about 3 or 4 mph<P>  so if you could just pop the clutch, the car would have to go from 0 to 3mph instantainiously! 3 mph doesnt sound like much, but 0 to 3 mph in 0.000000 seconds equals infinite acceleration. Infinite acceleration is impossible.<P>  Its almost as if your engine is moving down the street by itself, hovering along at 3mph and the rest of your car is sitting there at 0mph, and you need to latch onto the engine as it goes by.<P>  You need to latch on smoothly, gradually, not all at once. Dumping the clutch in first gear would be like having your motor slam into the back of your car at 3mph. In fact, if you pop the clutch in first gear, thats just what it feels like: Wham! the car jerks a foot or two, and then the engine stalls.<P>  By letting the clutch pedal up what you are doing is appling pressure to the flywheel, the more you let it up, the more pressure is applied. Think of it as pushing on the back of your car with your hands. If you want to push your car you put your hands onthe  back of it and apply pressure increasingly, you dont start 20 feet behind the car and get a running start with both arms out in front of you and slam yourself into the back of the car.<P>  as you let the clutch pedal up, more and more pressure is applied and there is more and more force pushing the car forward. At a certain point the car will start to move. You still cant just let it out all the way (you cant go from 1 mph to 3 mph instantainiously either) you have to keep letting it up smoothly as the car gains speed.<P>  the cheaters way, or the panic way, is to rev the gas pedal up. This only makes matters harder for the clutch. Now it doesnt have to go from 0 to 900 rpm, it has to go all the way up to 2000 or 3000 rpm, slipping all the way!<P>  The best way to practice starting off is to keep your foot off the gas pedal, let the clutch up till you see the tach drop a bit or hear the engine slow down, the car will start moving, let it up a little more, and continue till the pedal is up all the way. Then give it some gas. this is a slow way to start but it will give you the best 'feel' for the clutch, and will put the least wear and tear on it. Once you get that feeling and your foot knows where the clutch starts to engage, you can give it a little gas AFTER the car starts moving a little.<P>even in normal driving, on a level road or slightly downhill, if Im not in a hurry I still do this often, let the clutch out all the way before I give it any gas.<P>   As for taking off uphill, its easy.<P> EASY EASY EASY!!! forget all that handbrake stuff. you will never need the handbrake on any hill. to takeoff up hill, all calm and peacefull like - with no fear of ever stalling it, or rolling backwards even a thousands of an inch, heres what you do:<P>  keep your right foot on the brake, holding the car from rolling backwards. in first gear let the clutch out slowly and watch the tach. It will normally be at 900 rpm at idle or so. Let the clutch out till the tach drops down to 600 or 500 rpm, still holding the brake with your right foot.<P>   Here is the wonderfull thing about that electronic fuel injected engine back there. It will sense that the idle speed is dropping and it will let more air in with the IAC and the injectors will fire more fuel in, and it will try to get the rpms back up to 900 all by itself. You dont need to push the gas, you dont need no stinking handbrake.<P>  When you have dropped the tach to 600rpm take your right foot off the brake pedal, the force the clutch is applying will prevent the car from rolling backwards, it might even be enough to start moving the car forwards up the hill already. Put you right foot on the gas pedal, give it a little gas and then let the clutch pedal up the rest of the way like you normally would.<P>  BUT KEN, you will all say, arnt you slipping the clutch when you do this? arnt you holding the car on the hill with the clutch? Isnt that bad?<P>  well you will be slipping the clutch a little more than you usually do, but its okay cause you dont have to stop on an uphill all that often. Dont sit there at a red light holding the car with the clutch like that, hold the car with the brake, and then when its clear to go, takeoff like I explained above.<P> try to hold the car like this as little as possible - 2 or 3 seconds is okay - take your time, build your confidence - watch the tach and you will be able to do it the first time, and every time!<P>  As for the rest of your questions, the rest of the gears. For normal driving think of each gear as having a range. I dont know what they are on a fiero, but use the tach. When you are speeding up wait till the tach gets to about 2500 rpm for normal acceleration, then upshift. If you want to accelerate faster wait it it hits 3000. If you are really speeding up (on-ramp for example) wind it up to 4000 rpm).<P>You dont have to push the clutch pedal slowly for the rest of the gears, push it down, shift, let it up. When up shifting it shoud take about a second or less.<P>  to shift back down, when you are slowing down and the tach drops to 1200 or so, shift down to the next lower gear. If you want to, just tap the gas pedal a little between gears to rev the engine up, dont waste your time trying to get the engine to a certain rpm on the tack, when the clutch pedal is on the floor and you are downshifting, just give the gas pedal a friendly little tap.<P>You dont have to downshift to each gear if you are stopping - just leave it in whatever gear you are in till the engine gets down to about 900 rpm, then push the clutch to the floor and 'freewheel' the rest of the way while you brake to 0mph. If you are gonna coast along a bit, put it in neutral and you can let the clutch pedal up until the light turns green or you are clear to go again (if you dont feel like sitting there holding the clutch pedal down for a long light).<P>when downshifting its best if you wait till the car comes to a complete stop before shifting the tranny into first. If you dont stop all the way and want to go again, use 2nd gear.<P>  for going around a corner in the city, whatever gear you are in when you approach the corner, push the clutch all the way to the floor and shift into 2nd gear about halfway around the corner. When you get around the corner let the clutch back out. (Note Im talking about street corners here, not bends in the road).<P>  One last thing (whew!). If you stop at a red lite and you try to take off and the car stalls, and you try again and it stalls again, i can guarentee you - you have accidentally shifted into 3rd gear instead of 1st. Everyone does this once in a while. If you stall the car taking off, pull it back into neutral and then make sure you put it into first.<P>  Ok, one more last last thing. The way most people burn out their clutch is by resting their left foot on the clutch pedal while driving, not realizing they are pressing on it slightly, which causes the clutch to slip.  when you are driving along in one gear - take your foot off it completely and rest your foot on the floor.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Taijiguy||05-18-2001||11:09 AM||||I <I>think</I> what SlammedFiero was referring two are two techniques, one called "heel/toe" and the other "double clutching"<BR>To understand these, it helps to know their purpose.<BR>One thing many people don't realize about driving a standard is when they downshift, if they just let the clutch out, it causes the transmission and drivetrain to pull the engine RPM's up. The problem with this is specifically that it completely stresses the engine. You are in essence applying torque to the engine, which applies stress in reverse to what the engine is designed for. This is a good way to tear up an engine. <BR>The other is in differing transmission gear speeds. When you downshift, you have to remember that the input shaft will need to be spinning alot faster to make up the difference. So double clutching is used to ease these stresses. <BR>Heel/toe is used when double clutching, and the barake must be applied at the same time.<BR>So, in simple terms, the techniques go like this:<BR><B>Double clutching:</B><OL TYPE=1><BR><I>This is done of course, when the car is in motion, and you're downshifting.</I><BR><LI>Depress the clutch pedal<BR><LI>Slip the trans into neutral<BR><LI>Release the clutch pedal<BR><LI>Rev the engine a couple thousand RPM's (this is purely experience and feel)<BR><LI>Depress the clutch again<BR><LI>Slip the transmission into the lower target gear<BR><LI>Bring the engine RPM's up<BR><LI>Release the clutch (as in, ease it out)<BR></OL><BR><B>Heel/toe:</B><OL TYPE=1><BR><I>This is done when entering a corner, or stopping, and the brakes must be applied while double clutching. This allows you to operate the brake and the accelerator at the same time</I><BR><LI>Apply the brake with the heel of your right foot<BR><LI>Allow the toe of the right foot to swing to the right to tap the accelerator<BR></OL><BR>(There are several other uses for this, as some of the other racers here can tell you, but for the purposes of this thread, I'm only listing the one)   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 05-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Indiana_resto_guy||05-18-2001||11:29 AM||||WHOH! . . . . <BR>Way too much advice!<BR>Just get in and drive, keep take offs down to as low a rpm as you can-great way to learn a clutch.<BR>Try to keep on flat surfaces at first, then do inclines as they can be very tricky, each one is different.<BR>When stopping, if that's what you want to do the drive force needs to stop ie: press in the clutch. If going slow pick the correct gear, like if the car starts to buck and shiver (around 1500 RPM from Oldliar -not this time! LOL) pick a lower gear. The rest will follow shortly.<BR>Lots of luck!||||reg||1
Z||000013||Ken Wittlief||05-18-2001||01:29 PM||||I have to disagree onthe double clutching thing.<P>double clutching USE to be necessary when downshifting way way back before transmissions had synchronizers (they called them crash box trannys).<P>but that is what the synchronizers in the trannys are in there for, so you dont have to doubleclutch on a down shift. If you want to reduce the wear on the synchros, when downshifting, push the gearshift partway into the lower gear, you will feel a slight resistance, thats the synchro starting to spin up the lower gear, then tap the gas and you will feel the shift lever drop right into the lower gear when the rpms of the gears match.<P>  come on guys, seriously, double clutching a modern synchromesh transmission?!?! Put you right foot on the gas and the brake as you are going around the corner?!?! I gotta call you on these, for someone just learning to drive a stick that is bad advice. Let the guy learn how to drive the thing first, then if he wants to know about racing techniques, or how to drive a tranny with no synchros in it, or how to get 400,000 miles out of a tranny and clutch - thats stuff he might be ready for about a year from now.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Spektyr||05-18-2001||04:11 PM||||I taught my ex-fiance how to drive stick in the Nissan PU I used to have.  As near as I can tell, that truck's transmission and clutch are indestructable.  (A few times I goofed up, and slipped the clutch too much on a hard start, overheated it and it felt slippery for a few minutes.  But no permanent effect was ever noticed.)<P>Anyway, my teaching method is to get to a big empty parking lot (no need to worry about steering), and just practice getting moving in 1st gear.  With my ex, she got really frustrated but I knew she couldn't really tear the truck up and I told her so.  After she'd gotten the hang of getting the truck going, and done a few 1st-2nd and 2nd-3rd shifts, I just drove us home and I gave her the keys.  I told her to go for a drive and if she got in a situation where she couldn't afford to stall the truck, to simply put the gas to the floor and pop the clutch.  Her not freaking out is more important than me getting all 60,000 miles out of the rear tires (not that I was going to with my driving style anyway).<P>This goes along with what I belive to be one of the most important things about learning stick.  Not having the teacher in the car anymore than absolutely necessary.  If the car lurches and stalls or squeals the tires, or whatever, it's not nearly as annoying if you're the only one being thrown around inside.<P>I learned from my mom... she threw a fit when the car lurched.  I never did get the hang of it until I 'borrowed' the car one night and drove around the block a few times.<P>My learner philosophy of "When in doubt, tach it up and pop the clutch" may not be ideal, but it's a lot better than rolling backwards down a hill or getting rear-ended because the moron behind you at the light wasn't paying attention.  (some people see you move 3 feet and then just stop looking out the window as they pull forward.)<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?<p>[This message has been edited by Spektyr (edited 05-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||EdsB52||05-18-2001||06:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>I have to disagree onthe double clutching thing.<P>double clutching USE to be necessary when downshifting way way back before transmissions had synchronizers (they called them crash box trannys).<P>but that is what the synchronizers in the trannys are in there for, so you dont have to doubleclutch on a down shift. If you want to reduce the wear on the synchros, when downshifting, push the gearshift partway into the lower gear, you will feel a slight resistance, thats the synchro starting to spin up the lower gear, then tap the gas and you will feel the shift lever drop right into the lower gear when the rpms of the gears match.<P>  come on guys, seriously, double clutching a modern synchromesh transmission?!?! Put you right foot on the gas and the brake as you are going around the corner?!?! I gotta call you on these, for someone just learning to drive a stick that is bad advice. Let the guy learn how to drive the thing first, then if he wants to know about racing techniques, or how to drive a tranny with no synchros in it, or how to get 400,000 miles out of a tranny and clutch - thats stuff he might be ready for about a year from now.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I agree that the double-clutch technique for a new driver is too advanced, however I disagree with the notion that it is uneccessary to double clutch synchronized trannies, especially if you don't have the luxury of rebuilding it every time you drive (ex: race car). What if you're out racing around on the streets and you want to carry as much speed into the corner as possible? Well, you brake late, downshift into a gear that will result in high engine RPM's (due to the ratio between the new transmission gear and the speed of the vehicle), and stab the apex. If you simply jam the shifter into the new, lower gear, you will quickly waste your synchros, especially on a 13-17 year old car. Will the gear engage with force and without double-clutching? Yes, at least for a while. Soon it will not. In fact, if you look at threads posted about Getrag trannies, people often complain about the inability to downshift into first gear, even when stopped.<P>Besides, absent the wear and tear issue, wouldn't you rather have your pressure plate relaxing onto a clutch disk that has about the same RPM? In racing, everyone knows that a smooth driver is a fast driver. Again, for someone learning how to drive a stick, this concept is way too advanced, but for the matter of argument, I often double-clutch my vehicles when racing. I know that it is not easy to find someone to rebuild a Getrag, and I don't know what a rebuild on a 1994 Camaro 6 speed costs (nor do I want to find out).||||reg||1
Z||000016||88formula||05-18-2001||09:23 PM||||Don't give up on learning the stick. Everyone had to learn how it’s done like you are learning now. One thing I will say since everyone here has already explained how to drive one is that once you learn how to drive that stick intuitively you will not want to ever go back to an automatic sports car again. To me a sports car is not a true sports car unless it has a standard transmission.<P>I had to learn how to drive a stick with a 1000 lb. plow on the front of an old Chevy truck with a three speed transmission. <P>Just don’t rest your foot on the clutch pedal and don’t lug the engine.   <P><BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||jaguiler||05-18-2001||11:15 PM||||I learned to drive standard a while ago....maybe 7 years ago on a girlfriends MR2.....  It sounded like a lawn mower and as far as I know the car was OK after taht....but it sure did make lots of noise!<P>------------------<BR><BR>______<BR>__|aguiler||65.8.46.102||reg||1
Z||000018||Will||05-18-2001||11:46 PM||||Smooth is good. Smooth is fast.<P>Double clutching is unnecessary and obsolete. Rev-matching shifts accomplishes the same thing.<P>Heel-toeing is almost impossible in a Fiero<P>A Fiero <I>can</I> be left foot braked. You're just about sideways when you do it, however.<P>I don't know what Taijiguy was smoking, but his explanation is <I>not</I> why double clutching is done. Failing to rev-match a downshift does <I>not</I> impose any undue stress on the engine.<P>Getrag's notoriously reluctant first gear is a caused by: a) the large gap between first and second, and b) the fact that first gear is extremely low.<BR>A) in a 2-1 downshift, the speed difference between 1 and 2 is much larger than the speed difference between 2 and 3-&gt; the 2-1 downshift requies the synchros to do more work than a 3-2 downshift.<BR>B) Because 1 is so low, the angular momentum of the clutch disk and input shaft have much more mechanical advantage on the synchronizer than the angular momentum of said parts has is 2 or 3.<BR>It is in fact easier to shift a Getrag into first when between 0 and 3 mph than at a dead stop.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||SilverFieroV6||05-19-2001||12:32 AM||||I learned how to shift useing an old motorcycle.  Its not excatly the same but you get the feel of the clutch catching the gears alot better than in a car.  The first time I got into a car I had no problems driveing a stick.  If you have to use a car the way i teach paople is to basically just learn to get the car rolling and the rest is personal preferance.  I know a lot of people that downshift while coming to a complete stop as I dont always downshift while stoping.  To get rolling the easiest way is just trial and error.  Press on the gas and let out the clutch at the same time.  But as mentioned before every car is different so you cant use the rule of equal amounts  but its close to that.  If you know of anyone that has an old motorcycle try that it was a cakewalk after I did that.<BR>Sam||||reg||1
Z||000020||Tigger||05-19-2001||12:55 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>Failing to rev-match a downshift does <I>not</I> impose any undue stress on the engine.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, I would think this would impose stresses into the tranny.  All those gears, and parts, that were designed to be driven by the motor are now all of a sudden driven by the cars wheels and momentum.  All of the driving forces, creating stresses are reversed which I don't know for sure if they are "good" for the tranny. ||||reg||1
Z||000021||Pontiaddict||05-19-2001||08:15 PM||||I agree with Will on the impossibility of heel-toeing a Fiero. (at least with the stock pedals)<P>Failing to rev match the motor might kill something if you drop it down into 3rd while doing 120, but I doubt it will break anythng revving the engine 1 or 2 grand from high idle. Unless you do it too quickly and break the tires loose.<p>[This message has been edited by Pontiaddict (edited 05-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||EdsB52||05-19-2001||08:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B>Smooth is good. Smooth is fast.<P>Double clutching is unnecessary and obsolete. Rev-matching shifts accomplishes the same thing.<P>Heel-toeing is almost impossible in a Fiero<P>A Fiero <I>can</I> be left foot braked. You're just about sideways when you do it, however.<P>I don't know what Taijiguy was smoking, but his explanation is <I>not</I> why double clutching is done. Failing to rev-match a downshift does <I>not</I> impose any undue stress on the engine.<P>Getrag's notoriously reluctant first gear is a caused by: a) the large gap between first and second, and b) the fact that first gear is extremely low.<BR>A) in a 2-1 downshift, the speed difference between 1 and 2 is much larger than the speed difference between 2 and 3-&gt; the 2-1 downshift requies the synchros to do more work than a 3-2 downshift.<BR>B) Because 1 is so low, the angular momentum of the clutch disk and input shaft have much more mechanical advantage on the synchronizer than the angular momentum of said parts has is 2 or 3.<BR>It is in fact easier to shift a Getrag into first when between 0 and 3 mph than at a dead stop.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>While I agree that double-clutching has no effect on the engines wear, and that rev-matching eliminates disparities between the clutch and pressure-plate/flywheel, I do feel that jamming a transmission down into a gear without matching the RPM's of the synchros to the gears does create accelerated wear to the transmission, especially the synchros.  Now if you're racing on the track with a vehicle that will undergo often rebuilding, who cares. Or if you're driving a road car under normal conditions (not thrashing) then it is also uneccessary to double-clutch. The only time I double-clutch on the street is if I'm driving hard into a corner, braking late, and want a smooth transition into my new, lower gear. I agree with your assessment of the Getrag and first gear. And generally, even when racing, you would have no need to downshift into first gear unless you had to stop. Even the tightest of corners could be best handled with second gear.||||reg||1
Z||000023||maryjane||05-20-2001||12:17 AM||||Man, this brings back bad memories of trying to teach my kids how to drive a std.(long time ago). Finally showed them the basics, turned em loose on a BIG industrial parking lot, got out of the car and let them each find their own way that they were comfortable with and successful at. As you can see from all the above replies,it's kinda like sex,everyone has their own way of doing it.||||reg||1
Z||000024||ArchMagi||06-22-2001||10:09 PM||||A friend (older than me) of mine wanted to drive my previous car (70's bug) he said he knew how to drive a stick.  Well, stupid me, he diden't and in the process took about a 1/2 inch of clutch off.  He was jumping like a jackrabbit!<P>I do admit, it was quite funny watching him  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000025||Ferrari_cdn||07-16-2001||02:18 PM||||I am new to standard vehicles, although I did try using a stick once with my sister's '99 Sunfire (before she sold it away).<P>I was able to get the car moving, after a few stalls.  I was able to get the feel, and Im confident that I can handle the stick with the Fiero.<P>I am asking if there is any differences between a '99 Sunfire, or any new car, compared to a Fiero.  I mean, I found it very easy to shift from gear to gear without much difficulty, and I've read in some threads here that sometimes its a struggle to switch gears.<BR>I wanted to know whats the point in double clutching, because you say you press the clutch, shift to neutral, release, rev the engine up, press again, select the lower gear, rev up again and release clutch....I was wondering why, if you press the clutch, rev up the engine, then release at the appropriate gear, there wouldn't be much difference, would there?<BR>(I'm not going to double clutch, I'm going to master the stick first.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)<P>I see that you say if you don't want to freak, just floor the pedal and pop the clutch, won't that stall the engine because the car isn't moving, or the engine will have enough power to force the tires to spin, right?  (I'm not looking at leaving marks everywhere here  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)  BUt also wouldn't that blow the engine because when you punch the gas, the RPM will shoot up?||||reg||5
Z||000026||Ferrari_cdn||07-17-2001||03:20 AM||||*bump*<P>sorry.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000027||Pontiaddict||07-18-2001||01:32 AM||||1: There is a big difference between the Fiero and a Sunfire. Mainly the clutch is trickier to modulate. It's not a problem, it just takes some getting used to. If the shifter on a Fiero is hard to move, try lubing the cable. Before the junk new cables I bought went bad, the car almost shifting itself, the shifter movement was so easy.<P>Double clutching isn't needed. It's used to get the internals of the transmission moving at the same speed so it's easier to shift, but modern transmissions really don't need that. There's also heel-toe shifting that's used to eliminate weight shifting when you downshift going through turns. Some of the autocrossers could go into more detail than I could about that though.<P>Revving the motor and poping the clutch might stall the motor, but usually wont if the rpm's are high enough and the motor actually producesdecent amounts of torque. It isn't good for the car but unless you overrev the motor you won't blow it up. You'll probably go through clutches like tissues during flu season though.||||reg||1
Z||000028||artherd||07-18-2001||02:34 AM||||I learned to drive stick shift in a 1987 Ford F150 pickup truck.<P>In hilly Northerd Wisconson.<P>In February.<P>Did I mention it was a 2wd only?<P>Wasn't quite uphill both ways, more like DOWNhill both ways :)<P>Seriously, it's a dance and an art. Pay as much attention to us here as you would to someone telling oyu how to paint (that is to say, a tiny bit of what's been said here is good general practice, you need to put a brush on paper. Yes.  After that, ignore us and do what feels right to you.  You will know if you are shifting properly if you are driving ***smoother*** than an automatic transmission.<P>PS: remenber when I said dance? the dance is between the gears, and the engine's revs.  The art is in matching them all the time, perfectly :)<P>ENJOY!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000029||Butter||07-18-2001||10:39 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroLisa:<BR><B>He lived on a road that was shaped like a U, with 2 hills going up on either side, he lived at the bottom of the hill. The entire road was his family so not much traffic.<BR>He made me get in the car and just practice starting uphill forever. <P>a</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I wouldn't have believed there would have been this many replies to this topic but I thought I'd put in my 2 cents worth.  Now I lived on a farm so everything was a stick shift unless you's rich or stupid.  I started out keeping up the tractor and trailer for the folks loading the hay in the fields.  Now FieroLisa has got the right idea if you want to learn how to drive a stick well.  Just don't let the RPM's go too high and don't let them go low enough to jerk or stall the engine.  The next step I would recomend to improve from what her advise is would be to practice parrallel parking in between two cars on a hill and only pull the car up once to put it in line with the other parked cars.  If you learn how to do that smoothly I'd say Your better than most.  Now practice, practice, practice.<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000030||Posthumane||07-18-2001||12:43 PM||||I would just like to disagree with the post that said heel-toeing in a fiero is impossible. I do it on almost every downshift. The difference is I don't actually use my heel, but rather the side of my foor (so i guess it isn't really heel-toe, but same effect). Since the gas pedal on the fiero is single bolted near the bottom (or atleast on my car) you kinda have to tilt your foot at an angle and hit the bottom of the gas with the side of your foot. Luckily my feet are already twisted at that angle, so it's all good.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000031||Formula||07-18-2001||01:25 PM||||I stalled a twin turbo stealth because I didnt want to give it gas lol.....<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigformulagt.jpg"> ||||reg||1
